ism, unorganized; Candidate D responds to unexpected events adequately, concentrates very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, hot temper, know-it-all attitude, moody, loner.\n\nWe need to decide our recommendation. We should consider all candidates, including A and B, which have not been discussed much yet. The shared mental model says still need to discuss A and B. So we should bring them in. Perhaps we can argue that Candidate A's reliability and positive atmosphere outweigh his communication difficulty? Or Candidate B's conscientiousness and stress handling are strong, but his uncooperative attitude is concerning.\n\nWe need to make a focused contribution: maybe we can suggest that Candidate A should be considered because of 100% reliability and positive atmosphere, and that his communication difficulty may be mitigated, while other candidates have more severe crew cooperation issues. Or we could argue that Candidate B's conscientiousness and stress handling make him a strong candidate, but his uncooperative nature is a problem.\n\nWe need to reference the current discussion: Agent 2 recommended D, Agent 1 recommended C. We can say we see strengths in both but also bring A and B into comparison. Perhaps we can propose that Candidate A is the best overall because of reliability, attention, quick correct decisions, calm, and positive crew atmosphere, despite communication difficulty. However we need to consider that we have not yet discussed his communication difficulty in depth. Our notes say difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education. Those are concerns.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, but pretentious, wrong tone, can be grumpy, uncooperative. That seems serious.\n\nCandidate C: strong situational awareness, leadership, but unfriendly, not open to new ideas, eats unhealthily, not good at taking criticism, unorganized.\n\nCandidate D: problem solving, responsibility, concentration, but hot temper, know-it-all, moody, loner.\n\nWe need to decide which candidate best balances. Perhaps Candidate A's reliability and positive atmosphere is strong, but his communication difficulty and egocentric may affect crew cooperation. However his egocentric may be less severe than hot temper or unfriendly. Also he is not willing to further education, which could be a problem for long-distance pilot needing ongoing training.\n\nCandidate B's conscientiousness and stress handling are strong, but his uncooperative attitude is serious.\n\nCandidate C's leadership and situational awareness are crucial for safety, but his unfriendly and not open to feedback is also serious.\n\nCandidate D's problem solving and responsibility are strong, but hot temper is serious.\n\nWhich is best? Hard. Perhaps we can argue that Candidate C's leadership and anticipation of danger outweigh his feedback issues, making him the best. But Agent 1 already recommended C. We could either agree with C, or propose A or B. To bring new perspective, we could argue that Candidate A's reliability and positive atmosphere make him a strong candidate, and his communication difficulty may be less severe than the other candidates' cooperation issues. So we could shift recommendation to A.\n\nWe need to reference evidence: Candidate A 100% reliable, creates positive atmosphere (our note). Also from Agent 2's notes: quick correct decisions, calm under pressure, technical understanding, excellent attention skills, puts concern for others above everything. Concerns: difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education.\n\nWe can argue that his reliability and concern for others, plus quick decision making, are critical, and his communication difficulty can be mitigated with training, whereas other candidates have more ingrained attitude problems (hot temper, unfriendly, pretentious). So we recommend Candidate A.\n\nThus our public message: I appreciate the arguments for C and D, but I think A's operational reliability (100% reliable, creates positive atmosphere) and his quick correct decisions, calm under pressure, technical understanding, excellent attention, and concern for others make him the best fit. While he has difficulty communicating ideas and is egocentric, these are less severe than hot temper or unfriendly attitudes, and can be addressed. Therefore I recommend Candidate A.\n\nWe need to include explicit facts: 100% reliable, creates positive atmosphere, quick correct decisions, calm under pressure, excellent attention skills, puts concern for others above everything. Also mention concerns: difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education.\n\nNow produce METADATA_JSON with vote \"Candidate A\"."}
